# An ALLOW-list, for the reason spelled out in bib-backend/.dockerignore: a
# forgotten deny-list entry ships a secret silently, a forgotten allow-list
# entry breaks the build loudly. .env.local exists in this directory today.
#
# .next/ matters here beyond size. A stale local build left by `npm run dev`
# would be copied into the builder stage, and Next reuses what it finds — so the
# image could ship a bundle compiled against whatever NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL
# that developer had exported, which is precisely the value this build exists to
# control. Excluding it is correctness, not hygiene.
*

!package.json
!package-lock.json
!next.config.ts
!tsconfig.json
!postcss.config.mjs
# ESLint config travels with the source because a build that decides to lint and
# finds no configuration fails on the missing config, not on the code.
!eslint.config.mjs
!src

# next-env.d.ts is deliberately absent: it is gitignored, `next build`
# regenerates it, and a copy from a developer's machine can only disagree with
# the Next version the build just installed.

# Re-excluded from inside the allowed tree — `*` above only matches top-level
# entries.
**/.DS_Store
**/node_modules
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.tsbuildinfo
